<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang='en'>
  <head>
    <meta charset='utf-8'>
    <title><%= content_for(:title) || 'Recourse' %></title>
    <meta name='viewport' content='width=device-width, initial-scale=1'>
    <meta name='color-scheme' content='light dark'>
    <%# A refresh Turbo is sent morphs the page in place and keeps the scroll; without these it
        replaces the whole body, which drops the caret, the scroll and any menu that was open.
        On every page rather than on the ones that broadcast today, so a model that starts to
        never has to be remembered here. -%>
    <meta name='turbo-refresh-method' content='morph'>
    <meta name='turbo-refresh-scroll' content='preserve'>
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <%= csp_meta_tag %>

    <%= yield :head %>

    <%# Everything the page is styled by: Bootstrap 6 and bh's rules for this gem's
        markup, served by bh's own engine. `Recourse.assets` is where, and a host
        serving those files from somewhere else says so there. -%>
    <link rel='stylesheet' href='<%= Recourse.asset 'css/bh.css' %>'>
    <%# The one thing these screens set that the house's stylesheet does not: system faces
        at a compact size, for pages dense with figures. 14px as rem, so a browser's own
        text-size setting still scales it. After the stylesheet, since both are `:root`. -%>
    <style>
      :root {
        --bs-body-font-family: helvetica, verdana, arial, sans-serif;
        --bs-body-font-size: 0.875rem;
      }
    </style>
    <%# After the Bootstrap link, which is what makes either win: every selector involved
        is `:root`. The palette the host named, then the one the reader chose last time. -%>
    <%= theme_stylesheet_link %>
    <%= render 'recourses/scheme' %>
    <%= primary_color_style %>

    <%# Turbo, Bootstrap and a Stimulus application with every controller this markup
        names, started once under `window.Stimulus`. In the head, so a controller exists
        before the body's first `data-controller`. -%>
    <script type='module' src='<%= Recourse.asset 'js/bh.js' %>'></script>

    <%# The rest of the head is the host's: whatever it puts in a `recourses/head` partial
        — the icons its tab wears, a layer of its own over this one — is drawn here, from
        the host's own views or from a gem it loads, and nothing is guessed at where nobody
        has written one. Last, so a stylesheet of the host's outranks the rules above it and
        a module of its own runs after the application this one started. -%>
    <%= render 'recourses/head' if lookup_context.exists? 'recourses/head', [], true %>
  </head>

  <%# The reader's zone is told to the server here rather than from a page of its
      own: every screen the gem draws is one to read against their clock. -%>
  <body class='<%= class_names 'recourse-shell', 'recourse-expanded': expanded? %>'
        data-controller='timezone' data-timezone-storage-value='<%= Recourse::ZONE_STORAGE %>'>
    <%# First in the page, since a phone reads it first: the row of icons at the top,
        above the trail. From 768px the layout's grid seats it beside the content instead,
        under the navbar, whatever order it was written in. -%>
    <aside class='recourse-sidebar py-3 px-3'>
      <%= render 'recourses/sidebar' %>
    </aside>

    <%# A record's pages are drawn in its card whoever wrote the template, so the tabs and
        the buttons beside the trail stay the gem's and a host's own page is its body. Drawn
        here rather than where it is written, since the card is where the bare actions are
        said and the trail below is where they are read. -%>
    <% page = capture do %>
      <% if (record = card_record) %>
        <%= render layout: 'recourses/card', locals: { record: record } do %>
          <%= yield %>
        <% end %>
      <% else %>
        <%= yield %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>

    <nav class='navbar border-bottom'>
      <%# `row-gap` rather than a margin on the search: a margin is there whether the
          form wrapped or not, and on the line it shares with the breadcrumb it drops
          the field a few pixels below it. A row gap only exists between lines. Under a
          trail of more than one crumb a phone gives the form and the buttons rows of
          their own, which `recourse-trailed` is what the stylesheet reads. -%>
      <div class='<%= class_names 'container-fluid px-3 justify-content-start row-gap-2',
                                  'recourse-trailed': resource_breadcrumbs.size > 1 %>'>
        <%= render 'recourses/breadcrumb' %>
        <%= yield :actions %>
        <%= yield :search %>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <main class='py-4 px-3'>
      <%= page %>
    </main>

    <%= toasts(data: written_data) { |key, message| written_message key, message } %>
  </body>
</html>
